 The FiLM, SIREN, and CNN modules respectively provide subject-level conditioning, a continuous residual representation, and cross-frequency binaural refinement, jointly producing the final residual along one forward path. Unlike subject-specific model fitting, the conditional encoder compresses each subject's sparse binaural spatial observations into an embedding of shared dimensionality, enabling a single cross-subject model to express individual differences through FiLM modulation. This is the key interface connecting subject acoustic observations to the continuous neural field. In the validation-set CNN ablation, FSC E190 yields negative paired differences relative to FiLM-SIREN E130 on all eight metrics across three independent seeds, with all 24 upper confidence limits below zero; the three local-spectral metrics improve consistently in all three repeats (Table~\ref{tab:component-paired}). The result supports a stable incremental benefit from the CNN-refinement stage but does not isolate the causal contribution of each auxiliary loss.[m
